 fights, not the general election in Iowa"], "rationale": "Iowa has voted clearly Republican in recent presidential races, with results placing the state around six points to the right, and no polls, ratings, or market prices are available to move the race off that lean. The open-seat nature of 2028 keeps uncertainty wide.", "watch": "The emergence of each party's nominee, the first general-election polls in Iowa, or a shift in the national political environment could move this race quickly.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Iowa has drifted solidly Republican in presidential politics, with recent results putting the state roughly six points to the right of the country. The 2028 contest is an open race with the incumbent term-limited, and neither party has a settled nominee. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available yet, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisan lean.\n\nHeadlines so far concern nominating politics rather than the fall campaign: Democrats have set a 2028 primary calendar that leaves Iowa out of the early window, and both parties' fields are still forming. National coverage treats Iowa as contested ground for control of Congress. With no candidate-specific evidence, the race begins as a modest Republican lean with wide uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2447, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.291, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2906, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2896, "margin": -6.0}]}